"Boudewijn Waijers" <kroisos@REMOVETHISWORD.home.nl> writes:

> That's a strange way of reasoning. Life saving is much more valuable
> than a wish (if it is used). A wish has only a *chance* of saving
> your life, while your life being saved is 100% chance.

But with even a single wish you are much, much less likely to need
life saving.

>>  9.  Each turn spent carrying a quest artifact adds 3 turns
>>      (cumulative for all quest artifacts)
>
> Except for your *own* quest artifact, since the game (your quest leader)
> actually tells you to keep it with you.

No, including it. Sure, it is legally yours but in most cases it makes
the game much easier. A valkyrie with the Orb of Fate can't die except
for extreme carelessness. Ditto for a wizard with the Eye of
Mispelling. Sure, some of them are quite useless like the Heart of
Ahriman and the Misnamed Weapon of Muramasa but that is difficult to
quantify.

Though, perhaps there should be a grace-period of several hundreds of
turns to give the player time to return it to the Quest Leader without
being penalized for completing the quest.

I made the list based on my own playing style: what I find easy and
what difficult. Actually, I even forgot to add a penalty for
genociding monsters because that is one thing that I don't usually do. 

